<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Francia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> was the western kingdom created by the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843 CE, assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. It roughly corresponded to the western and central parts of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Accounts of the Carolingian Empire treat it as one of the successor kingdoms that emerged from the empire's fragmentation, and it later developed into the Kingdom of France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-francia.jpg" alt="West Francia" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: maix¿?; derivative work by Alphathon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Francia_843.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into <b>West Francia</b> under Charles the Bald, Middle Francia (from the Low Countries through Burgundy to Italy) under Lothair, and East Francia under Louis the German, ending the unified empire in practice even as the imperial title passed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As royal authority in <b>West Francia</b> weakened, the counts gathered rights over Quercy, Albi, Nîmes and Narbonne, and in the tenth century Raymond III Pons appears in some documents with the title of duke of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Burgundy took shape in the late ninth and early tenth centuries in the western part of the old Burgundian lands, the region of Autun, Dijon, Beaune and Chalon-sur-Saône that remained within <b>West Francia</b> when Provence and Upper Burgundy formed separate kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Rollo’s failed attack on Chartres in 911, King Charles the Simple of <b>West Francia</b> granted him lands around Rouen in return for defending the Seine against other raiders and accepting baptism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of France grew out of <b>West Francia</b>, the western share of the Carolingian Empire created by the Treaty of Verdun in 843.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun in August 843 divided the realm into three kingdoms: <b>West Francia</b> under Charles, East Francia under Louis, and Middle Francia, running from the North Sea to Italy, under Lothar, who kept the imperial title.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
